# Push notes

Whenever you commit and push to GitHub for this project, include an **overall
summary of what this push contains** — not a file dump, a short plain-language
note of the work shipped.

## Where the note goes

1. **Commit message body** — always. After the subject line, 2–6 bullets or
   short paragraphs covering the overall change set.
2. **GitHub** — when the push is meaningful (feature, fix, deploy-related,
   or first snapshot of a session), also publish the same summary on GitHub:
   - Prefer `gh release create` for milestone / named snapshots, or
   - `gh repo edit --description` only for one-line product blurbs, or
   - Paste into the PR body if the work went up via a pull request.
3. **Do not** rely only on chat history — the note must live on Git / GitHub.

## Note content

Cover: what changed overall, why it matters, and anything still deferred.
Skip exhaustive file lists unless a path is the point of the change.

## Author (when env is needed)

Prefer commit author `Wilsin Kuonadi <wilsinkarimunwkk@gmail.com>` via
`GIT_AUTHOR_*` / `GIT_COMMITTER_*` env vars. Do not rewrite old commits to
fix past author names unless the user explicitly asks.
